00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:18.190
Securing your public cloud
infrastructure is really hard.

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:20.190
You have to be aware of
possible vulnerabilities

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:23.140
throughout your tech stack, all
while preventing, detecting,

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.000
and responding to threats
in real time at scale.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.830
To remedy this,
you need a system

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:31.830
that can help track and
solve security threats.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.740
That system should keep
a comprehensive inventory

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.800
of all your assets in order to
find possible vulnerabilities

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.915
and weaknesses in
your architecture.

00:00:39.915 --> 00:00:41.790
It needs to detect when
bad actors are trying

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:44.130
to compromise your
systems and take action

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:46.035
to neutralize those problems.

00:00:46.035 --> 00:00:48.285
Wouldn't it be nice if there
was one central hub where

00:00:48.285 --> 00:00:51.270
we could gather all that info
so you can make decisions

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:53.170
on what to do next?

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:56.220
That's where Cloud Security
Command Center comes in.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.770
Cloud SEC is a
security and data risk

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:00.840
tool for Google
Cloud Platform that

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.450
helps you gather data,
identify threats, and act

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.120
on them before you suffer
business damage or loss.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:09.990
And now, Command Center is
available to all Google Cloud

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:11.040
customers.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.120
This hub makes securing your
cloud architecture much easier.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.790
Now you're using Google
Cloud Security tools

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:20.160
to help you find what
are potential weak points

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.020
in your system.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.080
It also locates actual activity
that's potentially malicious,

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.650
identifying and detecting
threats for you.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:30.720
And Cloud SEC comes
with the capability

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.080
to trigger either single
actions or entire workflows,

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.600
so you can respond to those
threats and vulnerabilities

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:37.770
automatically.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:39.750
One of the features
I'm most excited about

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.960
is the ability to
turn back time.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.390
After you detect a
vulnerability or a threat,

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:47.160
you might want to
restore your system back

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:50.100
to a previous
known secure state.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.790
Command Center lets
you dial back the clock

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:55.313
so you can be a true time lord!

00:01:55.313 --> 00:01:57.480
And because Cloud Security
Command Center isn't just

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.490
for Google Cloud,
we also integrate

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.950
with Partner
Solutions to help you

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.830
find vulnerabilities and threats
through third party tooling.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:06.510
To export data to
those partners,

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:08.710
you can use the data
for whatever they need,

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:10.650
and supporting threat
response actions

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:13.050
you might need which aren't
built into Google Cloud.

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.000
All this info can be
consumed by the built

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.250
in UI or from the
API, so you're not

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.320
locked in to the native
security products that

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.627
are pre-connected to Cloud
Security Command Center.

00:02:22.627 --> 00:02:24.960
This means you can use our
tools or the ones you already

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:25.770
know and love.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.090
Either one works.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:29.215
You can make your own tools
for consuming this data

00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:30.673
or you can bring
in your favorites,

00:02:30.673 --> 00:02:32.670
so you can holistically
get the benefits of all

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:35.220
the different detections.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.020
To see a full example
of this in action,

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:38.910
check out the full
talk below and you'll

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:41.790
see a demo of how, with the
specific tools and partner

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:44.760
integrations that
Cloud SEC has built in,

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.370
you can help secure
your cloud solutions.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:49.920
In that demo, Andy walks
through using the web UI

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.860
to hone in on a threat, find
what instances it impacted,

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.620
and take actions to clean up
those vulnerabilities using

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:56.700
Cloud Functions.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.080
If you want to
get started today,

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.122
you can head over to the
Google Cloud Marketplace

00:03:00.122 --> 00:03:03.150
to enable Cloud SEC through
a self-serve onboarding.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:05.340
Right away, you'll be able
to use a suite of tools

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.350
to start securing and
gathering information

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.630
about your cloud instances.

